## 12. PARLIAMENTARY SYSTEM

**[SYL: GS2-A.1] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 12]**

### 12.1. Features of Parliamentary System
- **Nominal and Real Executives**: President (nominal), PM & Council of Ministers (real)
- **Majority Party Rule**: PM is leader of majority party in Lok Sabha
- **Collective Responsibility**: Art. 75(3) - Council collectively responsible to Lok Sabha
- **Political Homogeneity**: Ministers usually from same party/alliance
- **Dual Membership**: Ministers must be members of Parliament
- **Leadership of Prime Minister**: PM is primus inter pares
- **Dissolution of Lower House**: President can dissolve Lok Sabha on advice of PM

### 12.2. Parliamentary vs Presidential System
| Feature | Parliamentary (India, UK) | Presidential (USA) |
|---------|---------------------------|-------------------|
| **Executive** | Dual (Nominal + Real) | Single (President) |
| **Responsibility** | Responsible to Legislature | Not responsible to Legislature |
| **Separation of Powers** | Less separation, more coordination | Strict separation |
| **Tenure** | Depends on confidence of House | Fixed term |
| **Dissolution** | Lower House can be dissolved | No dissolution of Congress by President |
| **Accountability** | Direct | Indirect |

