## 13. FEDERAL SYSTEM

**[SYL: GS2-A.1] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 13]**

### 13.1. Federal Features of Indian Constitution
- Written Constitution
- Division of Powers (7th Schedule)
- Supremacy of Constitution
- Rigidity (in certain provisions)
- Independent Judiciary
- Bicameralism

### 13.2. Unitary Features
- Strong Centre
- Single Constitution
- Single Citizenship
- Integrated Judiciary
- All-India Services
- Governor appointed by Centre
- Emergency Provisions
- Art. 356 (President's Rule)
- Parliament's power to reorganise states

### 13.3. Quasi-Federal Nature
K.C. Wheare described Indian Constitution as "federal system with a unitary bias" or quasi-federal. Granville Austin called it "cooperative federalism" in practice.

---


---

## 14. CENTRE-STATE RELATIONS

**[SYL: GS2-B.1] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 14]**

### 14.1. Introduction
- **Federal Structure**: Constitution of India is federal in structure, divides powers (legislative, executive and financial) between Centre and States
- **Integrated Judiciary**: No division of judicial power - single integrated judicial system enforces both Central and State laws
- **Need for Coordination**: Though supreme in their respective fields, maximum harmony and coordination essential for effective operation of federal system
- **Three Dimensions**: Legislative relations, Administrative relations and Financial relations (Art. 245-293)

### 14.2. LEGISLATIVE RELATIONS (ARTICLES 245-255)

#### 14.2.1. Territorial Extent of Central and State Legislation
- **Parliament**: Can make laws for whole or any part of territory of India (States + UTs + other areas)
- **State Legislature**: Can make laws for whole or any part of the State. Laws not applicable outside state except sufficient nexus between state and object
- **Extrateritorial Legislation**: Only Parliament can make extraterritorial legislation (laws applicable to Indian citizens and their property anywhere in the world)

**Restrictions on Parliament's territorial jurisdiction:**
- **UTs with regulations**: President can make regulations for peace, progress and good government of Andaman & Nicobar Islands, Lakshadweep, Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu and Ladakh (have force of Act of Parliament; can repeal/amend Parliamentary acts)

